UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'ZERO-YIELD, AND THE INSTRUMENT IS BAD RATHER THAN THE INDEX EMPTY — this is an R14 query-shape failure of a kind R14 does not name. Both requests returned HTTP 200 carrying a Cloudflare interstitial: "Just a moment... Enable JavaScript and cookies to continue" (5696 and 5753 bytes, persisted). A 200 THAT CONTAINS NO RESULTS IS NOT EVIDENCE OF ABSENCE, and scoring it as one would have been the same error batch 17 caught on JSTOR, where a 200 body was a bot interstitial. NOTHING IS CONCLUDED ABOUT WHAT HATHITRUST HOLDS. The owner ruling of 2026-09-18 records the instrument as known-bad for this purpose. || CORRECTED 2026-09-19: STATUS MISREPORTED, AND THE CORRECTION RUNS AGAINST ME. This note said the requests "returned HTTP 200 carrying a Cloudflare interstitial" and called it a false 200 of the shape batch 17 found on JSTOR. THE PERSISTED STATUS IS 403, NOT 200 — derive it from this session manifest, filtering on hathitrust. A 403 is an HONEST refusal: the server says it is blocking. A 200 carrying an interstitial is a DECEPTIVE success, which is precisely why the JSTOR case was worth recording. By misreading the status I upgraded a mundane block into the more alarming class and invoked a precedent that does not apply. WHAT SURVIVES UNCHANGED: the body is a Cloudflare interstitial, no results were returned, and NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT HATHITRUST HOLDS. What is withdrawn is the false-200 characterisation and the JSTOR analogy. Found by author_fidelity, which prints every non-2xx in a session manifest — a check that reads what a session RETRIEVED rather than what it SAID.' WHERE "exec_id" = 81;

