UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING, BUT IT IS NO LONGER A HYPOTHESIS — the record is now verified and the staged description was WRONG ON THE AUTHOR. Batch 17 staged it from REF-00980 deposited reference list as "Walter F." Open Library OL4653562M (retrieved this session) gives: "Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled", by_statement "[studies directed by] Felix Walter.", published by the DISABLED LIVING FOUNDATION, 346 Kensington High St, London, 1971, 60 pp. R15 says correct an over-claim rather than let a guess harden: the CORPORATE author is the Disabled Living Foundation and Walter directed the studies. Neither the reference list nor the catalogue is wrong; they name different things, and the staged row named only one. WHAT THE FULL TEXT WOULD SETTLE, AND WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ITS TIER: REF-01005 page 163 reports that Walter and Steinfeld INDEPENDENTLY found 1:16 over 20 feet accessible to at least 95 percent of wheelchair users. That convergence is currently held on Steinfeld testimony alone, so Walter is a single document counted once, not twice. Retrieving it would make the convergence real. NOT RETRIEVED: no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books; HathiTrust returned a Cloudflare interstitial and is unresolved rather than negative. NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW RETRIEVAL CLAIM, same defect as candidate 110. The re-description above says "no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books". GOOGLE BOOKS WAS NEVER ASKED — HTTP 429, daily quota exceeded. Open Library and Internet Archive stand and were re-derived. The bibliographic record, which is what this row is FOR, is unaffected: Open Library OL4653562M, Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 109;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING AND ITS DESCRIPTION IS NOW CORROBORATED BY A PRIMARY SOURCE RATHER THAN BY A REFERENCE LIST. Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0. BUT REF-01005 page 163 characterises it directly: "Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users. However, his sample was taken from wheelchair users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education center and the findings probably reflect the high standard of excellence in rehabilitation training that the subjects received as part of their program." SO THE 1957 FINDING IS 1:8, AND THE FIELD OWN 1979 ASSESSMENT IS THAT IT IS UNREPRESENTATIVE BECAUSE THE SAMPLE WAS ELITE-TRAINED. That is a selection-bias critique published eight years before the ADA, and it is itself first-class historical evidence about how the threshold literature was weighed. A WEB SEARCH SUMMARY ASSERTED THIS WAS A UNIVERSITY OF IOWA MASTER THESIS AND NONE OF ITS OWN RETURNED LINKS SUPPORTED THAT — the claim is discarded, not recorded (CLAUDE.md 5(c)). The institution is NOT established: REF-01005 says only "a pioneering rehabilitation-education center" and naming one would be invention. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW OWN RETRIEVAL CLAIM. The re-description above states "Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0". GOOGLE BOOKS 0 IS FALSE. That query returned HTTP 429 (daily quota exceeded) and was never asked; recording an unfired query as a null is the error batch 17 made on SafetyLit and is corrected here rather than left standing. THE OTHER THREE ZEROS HOLD and were re-derived, not assumed: Open Library numFound 0, Internet Archive 0 relevant, ERIC 0. EVERYTHING ELSE IN THE ROW IS UNAFFECTED — Elmer 1957 finding of 1:8 maximum, and Steinfeld 1979 dismissal of it as an elite-trained sample, come from REF-01005 page 163 and stand on a retrieved primary source.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 110;